They're shadows of a deeper underlying process that we just can't capture in a single clean snapshot.

02:52.160 --> 03:02.760
And that unsettling realization that our diagnostic tools might be showing us shadows instead of the actual bone is the precise paradigm shift this paper is applying to the very fabric of the universe.

03:03.180 --> 03:09.700
For the last century, physicists have demanded that their mathematical blueprints look exactly like the observable universe.

03:10.300 --> 03:20.180
If the math spits out something weird, something we can't touch or see, the immediate instinct has been to just, well, to throw the entire theory in the trash.

03:20.180 --> 03:27.780
And the foundational rule of standard quantum mechanics, the very first thing this paper targets, is something called the Hilbert Assumption.

03:28.580 --> 03:34.800
Before we even talk about gravity, we need to establish the ground rules of how quantum physicists do their daily accounting.

03:35.060 --> 03:35.160
Right.

03:35.400 --> 03:42.480
The paper states that standard physics demands the state space of any physical system must be a complex Hilbert space.

03:42.480 --> 03:44.380
So let's define the terms here.

03:44.920 --> 03:47.680
What exactly makes a mathematical space a Hilbert space?

03:47.780 --> 03:50.600
And why is standard physics so fiercely protective of it?

03:50.700 --> 03:50.940
Okay.

03:51.060 --> 03:55.880
To really grasp this, we need to go back to the 1920s to a mathematician named David Hilbert.

03:56.260 --> 04:00.980
When quantum mechanics was first being formalized by people like Heisenberg and Schrödinger,

04:00.980 --> 04:08.240
they realized that classical math, just tracking a particle's position and momentum with regular numbers, wasn't enough.

04:08.320 --> 04:10.040
Because things are super weird down there.

04:10.340 --> 04:10.720
Exactly.

04:11.160 --> 04:14.140
Quantum particles don't exist in single definite states.

04:14.440 --> 04:14.680
No.

04:14.780 --> 04:16.300
They exist in superpositions.

04:16.420 --> 04:19.420
They have a probability of being in many different states all at once.

04:19.700 --> 04:21.020
Right, like Schrödinger's cat.

04:21.120 --> 04:23.340
It's not alive or dead until you look.

04:23.440 --> 04:24.920
It's a weird combination of both.

04:25.060 --> 04:25.540
Precisely.

04:25.540 --> 04:31.920
So to describe a system that is a combination of many possible states, mathematicians realized

04:31.920 --> 04:35.680
they needed an infinite dimensional linear vector space.

04:36.300 --> 04:37.780
Okay, that sounds intense.

04:37.920 --> 04:39.840
Think of it like a three-dimensional grid, right?

04:39.880 --> 04:41.380
The x, y, and z axes.

04:41.920 --> 04:44.680
But extend it out to an infinite number of axes.

04:45.120 --> 04:50.360
Every possible state a quantum system could be in gets its own axis, its own coordinate direction

04:50.360 --> 04:51.800
in this massive space.

04:51.920 --> 04:52.100
Yeah.

04:52.280 --> 04:54.220
This mathematical arena is the Hilbert space.

04:54.300 --> 04:54.560
Okay.

04:54.560 --> 04:56.500
But it's not just a blank grid.

04:56.900 --> 05:01.820
It comes equipped with a very specific, highly rigid rule about how you measure things inside

05:01.820 --> 05:01.960
it.

05:02.040 --> 05:04.560
It has what's called a positive definite inner product.

05:04.780 --> 05:05.760
Okay, an inner product.

05:05.860 --> 05:07.260
Let me make sure I'm mapping this correctly.

05:07.420 --> 05:13.820
In standard geometry, if I have two vectors, like two arrows pointing in space, an inner product

05:13.820 --> 05:17.660
or a dot product is a way of multiplying them together to see how much they overlap.

05:18.280 --> 05:23.660
If it's a vector times itself, the inner product gives you the length of that arrow squared.

05:23.660 --> 05:25.140
That is exactly right.

05:25.380 --> 05:29.260
Now, in quantum mechanics, the vectors aren't pointing in physical space.

05:29.380 --> 05:30.980
They represent the state of the system.

05:31.340 --> 05:35.520
And the length squared of that state vector represents probability.

05:35.760 --> 05:36.260
Got it.

05:36.260 --> 05:42.360
The inner product is the mathematical engine that takes the abstract quantum state and spits

05:42.360 --> 05:45.980
out the probability that you will actually observe that state in a laboratory.

05:46.360 --> 05:48.600
And because it's a positive definite inner product?

05:48.600 --> 05:52.600
Because it is positive definite, it guarantees that no matter what vector you choose, no matter

05:52.600 --> 05:58.420
what crazy quantum state you calculate, the inner product, the probability, will always

05:58.420 --> 06:00.600
be a positive number or zero.

06:01.100 --> 06:03.080
But it can never, ever be negative.

06:03.360 --> 06:05.740
Which intuitively makes total sense.

06:05.880 --> 06:11.260
I mean, if I flip a coin, I can have a 50% chance of getting heads or a 0% chance if the

06:11.260 --> 06:12.400
coin is totally rigged.

06:12.500 --> 06:16.760
But I cannot possibly have a negative 50% chance of getting heads.

06:16.760 --> 06:20.220
A negative probability is a physical absurdity.

06:20.360 --> 06:21.980
It is a complete physical absurdity.

06:22.360 --> 06:26.660
And because it's absurd, the founders of quantum mechanics made a philosophical commitment.

06:27.360 --> 06:29.000
It wasn't just a mathematical choice.

06:29.200 --> 06:32.400
It was a deeply ingrained ontological belief.

06:33.020 --> 06:35.440
This is what Flyxion calls the Hilbert Assumption.

06:35.500 --> 06:36.620
The Hilbert Assumption.

06:36.660 --> 06:36.840
Right.

06:36.840 --> 06:42.700
It is the unspoken foundational dogma that the entire internal mathematical state space,

06:43.180 --> 06:48.980
every single gear, pulley, and vector inside the equations, must perfectly equal the observable

06:48.980 --> 06:49.880
physical reality.

06:50.060 --> 06:54.660
So the rule is, if I can't hold it in my hand, or at least measure it on a Geiger counter

06:54.660 --> 06:59.340
as a positive probability, it is absolutely not allowed to exist anywhere in the mathematics.

06:59.760 --> 07:00.080
Yes.

07:00.760 --> 07:03.560
The Hilbert Assumption declares a strict one-to-one mapping.

07:04.220 --> 07:06.960
Observable physics equals the entire state space.

07:07.400 --> 07:11.120
Every element of the math must be a candidate physical state.

07:11.460 --> 07:13.620
And this brings us to Flyxion's core premise.

07:14.720 --> 07:16.220
What if we break that mapping?

07:16.320 --> 07:18.340
What if we don't force them to be identical?

07:18.760 --> 07:19.140
Exactly.

07:19.320 --> 07:22.500
What if we separate the internal map from the external territory?

07:23.080 --> 07:28.420
Flyxion proposes that the internal mathematical representation, which the paper calls the admissibility

07:28.420 --> 07:36.560
manifold, or MM, can be vastly larger, wilder, and mathematically weirder than what we actually

07:36.560 --> 07:39.540
observe, which is the observable manifold, or MOBs.

07:39.540 --> 07:44.200
The paper introduces a really great piece of terminology for this specific error in physics.

07:44.200 --> 07:46.560
It calls it latent fundamentalism.

07:46.640 --> 07:49.340
And I want to linger on this because it's not just a physics problem.

07:49.460 --> 07:51.640
It's a profound philosophical trap.

07:51.760 --> 07:52.320
It really is.

07:52.320 --> 07:57.420
Latent fundamentalism is the error of granting ontological status, meaning believing something

07:57.420 --> 08:02.080
is physically, tangibly real to an internal coordinate just because it happens to appear

08:02.080 --> 08:03.840
in a successful mathematical model.

08:04.100 --> 08:05.120
It's a brilliant term.

08:05.440 --> 08:10.800
It perfectly diagnoses a cognitive bias that has plagued theoretical physics for a century.

08:11.440 --> 08:13.000
Think about how science usually works.

08:13.640 --> 08:18.240
You build a mathematical machine that successfully predicts the outcome of a complex experiment.

08:18.240 --> 08:18.800
Right.

08:19.160 --> 08:22.760
You run the numbers, and the machine tells you the electron will hit the screen exactly

08:22.760 --> 08:23.340
right there.

08:23.480 --> 08:25.780
You run the experiment, and it hits exactly there.

08:26.420 --> 08:31.600
The natural human instinct is to look inside your mathematical machine, point to a specific

08:31.600 --> 08:38.200
variable or a specific term in the equation, and say, because my machine correctly predicted

08:38.200 --> 08:44.300
reality, this specific gear inside the machine must literally exist in the physical universe.

08:44.460 --> 08:47.220
But Flyxion argues that is a massive logical leap.

08:47.220 --> 08:52.040
The internal machinery, the admissibility manifold, is just a space of histories, coordinates,

08:52.240 --> 08:52.780
and constraints.

08:53.280 --> 08:56.380
It doesn't have to map one-to-one with the observable outcomes.

08:57.320 --> 09:01.820
Let me ground this with a financial analogy, because I think this maps perfectly to how human

09:01.820 --> 09:03.760
beings actually run complex systems.

09:03.820 --> 09:04.420
I like this.

09:04.520 --> 09:04.740
Go ahead.

09:05.020 --> 09:06.720
Think of an accountant's corporate ledger.

09:07.140 --> 09:12.560
The internal ledger of a massive multinational corporation uses negative numbers all the time.

09:12.560 --> 09:17.300
It uses debts, liabilities, phantom placeholder accounts, depreciation curves.

09:18.120 --> 09:21.400
That internal ledger is the admissibility manifold.

09:22.160 --> 09:26.120
An accountant has to use negative numbers to balance the books and make the financial

09:26.120 --> 09:27.640
reality function over time.

09:27.880 --> 09:27.960
Right.

09:27.960 --> 09:33.920
But the actual money a customer can withdraw from the ATM, the observable manifold, must

09:33.920 --> 09:35.500
always be a positive number.

09:35.780 --> 09:38.780
You can't physically hold a negative $20 bill in your hand.

09:38.880 --> 09:41.120
That is an exceptionally precise analogy.

09:41.680 --> 09:46.080
The internal accounting absolutely requires negative numbers to function efficiently and

09:46.080 --> 09:49.120
to capture the full dynamical picture of the corporation's finances.

09:49.600 --> 09:51.760
The negative numbers are mathematically indispensable.

09:51.980 --> 09:52.200
Yeah.

