a negative probability.

11:11.920 --> 11:17.100
Wait, wait, so we literally have negative probability photons hiding in the standard

11:17.100 --> 11:18.040
equations of light?

11:18.200 --> 11:19.060
We absolutely do.

11:19.400 --> 11:24.100
They exist in the math to ensure the equations remain symmetric and respect the laws of relativity.

11:25.240 --> 11:31.300
But, especially before we get to the observable physical predictions, before we go to the ATM,

11:31.680 --> 11:37.060
as you said, a subsidiary condition known as the Gupta-Bluler condition is applied.

11:37.140 --> 11:37.860
Okay, what does that do?

